  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAfternoon Update: Latest radar scans continue to show light \nscattered rain showers across the southern Adirondacks this \nafternoon. Visible satellite imagery continues to show \nincreasing clouds from west to east across eastern New York and \nwestern New England this afternoon. This on track with the\nforecast from this morning and no changes were made to current\nforecast. Read below for more details from the previous \ndiscussion.\n\nPrevious Discussion: After a dry start to the day with some \npatchy fog, scattered showers and isolated T-storms are expected\nto develop this afternoon, as a cold front approaches from the \nGreat Lakes and SE Canada. Guidance continues to show this front\nweakening as it enters our area, so will only mention shower \nchances mainly for areas north/west of Albany. A few showers may\nget close to the Capital District towards evening as the front \nmoves in, so will mention 20% PoPs. Guidance still showing \nlimited instability with SBCAPE < 700 J/Kg from HREF, so will \ncontinue to only mention slight chance of thunder. \n\nThe decaying front shifts south of the area tonight into Thu, \nalthough much of the latest guidance has the remnants of the \nboundary only reaching SE PA/NJ before stalling with a wave of \nlow pressure developing along the old boundary and tracking east\nalong it on Thu. CAMs are especially aggressive indicating \nshowers from this wave making it far enough north into at least\nsouthern parts of our area (E. Catskills, mid Hudson Valley, \nTaconics, Litchfield CT). Global guidance is not as aggressive \nthough, but AI models do have showers south of Albany. NBM \nPoPs/QPF look too low, so have increased PoPs to likely for \nthese areas by Thu P.M. and used WPC QPF, which shows around \n0.25-0.50\" across the far southern part of the area. The threat \nfor any severe storms remains very low, as any appreciable \ninstability should be displaced well south of our region. As a \nresult of more clouds/showers around, temperatures look cooler \n(mainly 70s) and may need to be lowered further if trends hold. \nThe showers should taper off from west to east Thu evening as \nthe wave tracks into SE New England.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfternoon Update: No significant changes for this weekend from \nthe previous discussion, read below for more details. \n\nPrevious Discussion: The pattern looks to become more active by\nthe later half of the weekend into early next week, as a deep \nupper level trough settles in across the Great Lakes and \nNortheast. The weekend is coming into somewhat better focus now.\nSat looks like the drier day compared to Sun, although will \nmention chance PoPs south/east of Albany due to potential for \nsome showers along the northern periphery of a coastal wave \nexpected to track from the mid Atlantic coast to SE New England.\nThe more widespread showers should be south of our area based \non the current guidance. Sun is expected to be the wetter day, \nas forcing increases downstream of the upper trough axis with \ndiffluence aloft. With the main surface cyclone expected to \ntrack north into Canada, the system's occluded front should \nprovide low level focus for likely rounds of showers (and \nperhaps some T-storms) to move across our area. The threat of \nheavy rain has decreased, as most deterministic, AI and ensemble\nguidance have loosely consolidated indicating a closed low \ntracking north/west of our area. There is still spread in the \nexact track, but latest trends at least indicate lowering \npotential for heavy rainfall. Latest NBM showing a 15-35% prob \nof > 1.0\" rain ending Monday morning. Temperatures look to be \nbelow normal through early next week associated with the trough.",
